    animation: { visualBridge: 'A figure grasping at objects labeled "MINE" — each object dissolves as the figure realizes the "I" is empty. Not disappearing but RELAXING — the grip loosens, the figure becomes peaceful. Objects remain but the desperate clinging is gone.', educationalGoal: 'User sees: empty self → empty mine → cessation of grasping → peace. The practical liberation that follows from understanding emptiness.' },
    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'How does empty self imply empty "mine"?', a: '"Mine" is a relational concept: X belongs to ME. If "me" lacks inherent existence, the relationship "mine" can\'t be inherently established either. The possessor is empty → the possession-relationship is empty → "mine" is empty. The entire structure of ownership collapses at the inherent level.', realLifeExample: 'If a company doesn\'t inherently exist (it\'s just people, contracts, assets), then "the company\'s property" doesn\'t inherently belong to anything. Conventional ownership continues, but it\'s revealed as a dependent designation, not an inherent fact.' },
      { q: 'What is "pacification" (upaśama)?', a: 'The calming or cessation of mental afflictions — specifically ahaṃkāra (I-making: "I am this") and mamakāra (mine-making: "this is mine"). This isn\'t emotional suppression but the natural result of SEEING emptiness. When the basis for grasping dissolves, grasping naturally ceases.', realLifeExample: 'Realizing that a scary shadow is actually a tree: your fear (grasping at "danger") naturally ceases. You don\'t need to "suppress" the fear — it dissolves when the misperception is corrected. Emptiness similarly corrects the misperception of inherent self.' },
      { q: 'Is this recommending detachment?', a: 'Not cold detachment — FREEDOM from desperate clinging. You can still love your family, enjoy your work, and care about outcomes. But the SUFFERING caused by grasping ("I MUST have this," "I can\'t LOSE this") diminishes. It\'s the difference between holding something gently and clutching it desperately.', realLifeExample: 'A gardener who loves their garden but doesn\'t suffer when seasons change. They tend it carefully (conventional care) without demanding that flowers last forever (grasping). They enjoy what arises and accept what passes.' },
      { q: 'How does this connect to Ch.17\'s karma analysis?', a: 'Ch.17: actions are empty. Ch.18 v.2: the SELF performing those actions is empty, and "my actions" (mine-making applied to karma) is also empty. This deepens Ch.17: not just the WHAT (action) is empty, but the WHO (self) and the WHOSE (mine).', realLifeExample: 'Ch.17: "This deed is empty." Ch.18: "The doer is empty too, and \'my deed\' is a conventional designation." The emptiness extends from action to actor to ownership.' },
      { q: 'What are ahaṃkāra and mamakāra?', a: 'Ahaṃkāra: "I-making" — the construction of an inherent "I" from the aggregates. Mamakāra: "mine-making" — extending that illusory "I" to claim ownership over experiences, objects, and people. Both are forms of grasping rooted in the false belief in inherent self. V.2 targets both.', realLifeExample: 'Ahaṃkāra: "I am a successful person" (constructing identity from achievements). Mamakāra: "My success, my reputation, my career" (claiming ownership). Both cause suffering when threatened. V.2: see through both.' },
      { q: 'What is the soteriological significance?', a: 'This is where Madhyamaka philosophy becomes THERAPY. Understanding emptiness isn\'t just intellectual — it\'s LIBERATING. When you genuinely see that "I" and "mine" lack inherent existence, the grasping that causes suffering naturally loosens. Philosophy becomes the path to peace.', deeper: 'V.2 is arguably the most practically important verse in Ch.18 — it connects emptiness directly to liberation from suffering. The chain: empty self (v.1) → empty "mine" (v.2a) → cessation of grasping (v.2b) → peace (upaśama). This is the ENTIRE Buddhist path in one verse: wisdom (seeing emptiness) → cessation (of grasping) → liberation (peace). Every other verse in Ch.18 elaborates on this fundamental movement from insight to freedom.' },
    ],
